    {
      "text": "It's free for you to produce, free to refactor, and it is not a thing to get hung up on anymore. Humans no longer need to concern themselves with implementation.",
      "timestamp_sec": 247,
      "why": "The maximalist pole of the continuum, in LeFebvre's own words."
    },
    {
      "text": "The important thing is not the code, but the prompt and the guardrails that got you there.",
      "timestamp_sec": 247,
      "why": "States where the optimist camp thinks attention should move."
    },
    {
      "text": "Agents are actually compounding booboos, which is my word for errors, with zero learning and no bottlenecks and delayed pain.",
      "timestamp_sec": 294,
      "why": "Zechner's mechanism for why unreviewed agent output degrades over time."
    },
    {
      "text": "Non-critical code, sure, write slop ahead. Critical code, read every line.",
      "timestamp_sec": 338,
      "why": "The cautious pole, and already a task-routing rule rather than a blanket one."
    },
    {
      "text": "861% increase in code deletion per PR. So us together with AI agents, we love deleting code.",
      "timestamp_sec": 543,
      "why": "Headline stat from the Faros AI survey supporting the optimists."
    },
    {
      "text": "Anthropic also said that they're shipping eight times more code per quarter than in 2025.",
      "timestamp_sec": 543,
      "why": "Reported throughput figure from the company with the most AI-generated code."
    },
    {
      "text": "Same essay, 31% increase in PRs merged with no review at all, human or agentic. Don't do this. I beg of you.",
      "timestamp_sec": 619,
      "why": "The one practice Volkov flatly condemns, with a number attached."
    },
    {
      "text": "Same study, 242% increase in incident per PR. This kind of scary. The second study is also scary. Bugs per developer is up six times than 2025.",
      "timestamp_sec": 619,
      "why": "The counterweight data showing output gains are not free."
    },
    {
      "text": "The continuum is real. It's not about the people. It's about the task.",
      "timestamp_sec": 710,
      "why": "The talk's central correction and reusable reframe."
    },
    {
      "text": "He's not saying don't inspect your code. He's saying inspect the system, not every line.",
      "timestamp_sec": 753,
      "why": "Reconciles the two poles by distinguishing object-level from system-level review."
    },
    {
      "text": "I think the better question right now for all of us is what proof does this specific change need?",
      "timestamp_sec": 830,
      "why": "Replaces the binary question with the operational one."
    },
    {
      "text": "You read every line of authentication, money movement, permissions, and irreversible data.",
      "timestamp_sec": 868,
      "why": "The concrete, checkable boundary of the routing table."
    },
    {
      "text": "This is what Ryan Le Popo talks about. Build the system that builds the system because read spends your attention once, engineer makes the system remember.",
      "timestamp_sec": 900,
      "why": "Names the tradeoff between one-time attention and durable systemic proof."
    },
    {
      "text": "We used to check if Claude is doing the work right. With Fable, we check if Claude is doing the right work.",
      "timestamp_sec": 970,
      "why": "Captures the capability-driven shift in what review is even for."
    },
    {
      "text": "It's never felt so tempting to stop looking at code at all. But don't do this in production.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1009,
      "why": "Karpathy stating the anxiety from both ends in one sentence."
    },
    {
      "text": "Capability drift changes where proof belongs. It doesn't remove the requirement of proof.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1048,
      "why": "The talk's answer to \"does this survive the next model?\""
    },
    {
      "text": "But if the builder grades itself, you didn't remove the review, you hid it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1157,
      "why": "The sharpest critique of self-verifying agent loops."
    },
    {
      "text": "So again, loops don't remove judgment, but they do raise the stakes on where you put it.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1191,
      "why": "Positions loops as an amplifier of review placement, not a replacement."
    },
    {
      "text": "Not every line in 2026 needs your eyes. Every system still needs your judgment.",
      "timestamp_sec": 1266,
      "why": "The closing formulation of the whole argument."
    }
  ],
